WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
- [Narrator] Here's the G7,

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.470
a group of seven of the
world's advanced economies

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.830
including the US, UK, and Japan.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.020
And here's what some call the alternative

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.630
from the developing world, the BRICS,

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:17.630
formed by Brazil, Russia,
India, China, and South Africa.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.820
The BRICS is trying to
position itself as the voice

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.180
for emerging economies and
challenge western dominance

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.830
of global affairs.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.440
Its leaders are holding
a summit in Johannesburg

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.080
to discuss an historic expansion.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.610
More than 40 countries

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.640
including Saudi Arabia
and Iran want to join.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.810
- The expansion of BRICS

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.080
could definitely boost the
group's global influence.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:40.913
- [Narrator] Let's look

00:00:40.913 --> 00:00:43.590
at what a potential enlargement
means for the alliance

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.740
and its ambitions to shake
up the global political

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.780
and economic order.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.970
- The original term
BRIC was actually coined

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.830
by a Goldman Sachs banker,

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.700
grouping these really
different countries together

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.590
because they were so fast-growing.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.720
- [Narrator] The leaders
of Brazil, Russia, India,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.530
and China formalized their
relationship in 2009,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.950
and invited South Africa to
join the alliance a year later.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.470
- They decided to take
that idea in an effort

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:15.030
to create a bit of a political
and economic counterpoint

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.300
to what's perceived as
the western hegemony

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.270
of the global political
and economic spheres.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.650
- [Narrator] The BRICS countries together

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.800
make up more more than 40%
of the world's population

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.383
and almost 1/3 of the world's GDP.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.310
According to data from the
International Monetary Fund,

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:36.000
the bloc has already overtaken
the G7 nations' share

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.500
of the global economy

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.780
on the basis of purchasing power parity.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.190
That's where the purchasing power

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.800
of different currencies is equalized.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.570
Projections by Goldman Sachs

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.970
show the world's economic balance shifting

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.670
towards the BRICS and emerging economies.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.340
China is expected to overtake the US

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.280
as the world's largest economy by 2050,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.590
with India climbing to number three.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.040
- China and India have
experienced massive growth

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.800
since the BRICS were founded in 2009,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.580
and certainly have helped
them swell in importance

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.013
and remain relevant on the global stage.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.150
- [Narrator] China and Russia
are pushing to enlarged BRICS.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.590
Experts say Beijing is keen

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.317
to create an alternative
grouping to counter the US

00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:21.780
and the west on the world stage.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.290
- China has been bullish on
expanding BRICS for some time,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.630
and they're particularly
keen at the moment

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:31.350
because of the trade tensions
with the US, the US looking

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.160
to diversify its supply
chains away from China.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.570
- [Narrator] Russia sees an expansion

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.880
as a way to gain more international allies

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.333
following its full-scale
invasion of Ukraine.

00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:42.930
(footsteps pattering)

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.960
South Africa publicly
supports the expansion,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.990
as does Brazil, but experts
say behind the scenes,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.500
Brazil and India want a
more measured approach.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.600
- It seems that they are concerned

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.060
about the group leaning too autocratic,

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.320
so they want to have very
specific requirements

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.800
for admission to make sure
that there's a balance

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:05.910
amongst the members,

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:10.910
and that it doesn't become
sort of the autocratic G7.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.320
- [Narrator] More than 20
countries have formally applied

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.350
to become part of BRICS,
ranging from Saudi Arabia

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.100
to Ethiopia, Argentina, and Indonesia.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.860
Experts say Saudi Arabia
is a strong candidate

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.160
as a major economic and energy
power in the Middle East

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.100
with a growing role in regional diplomacy.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.160
Indonesia with the world's
fourth largest population

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.600
is another strong contender.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.090
- Indonesia being such a large economy

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.230
and important in the
electrification, clean energy tech,

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.200
electric vehicles, might
be a logical choice.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.850
- [Narrator] One reason
for joining the BRICS

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.450
is gaining access to the
bloc's New Development Bank.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.730
Since its founding in 2014,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.180
the bank has approved more
than $30 billion in loans

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.180
for development and
infrastructure projects.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.610
- Access to funding from this bank

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.550
which seemed to be having
more favorable terms

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.340
than a traditional development
bank like the World Bank

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.800
or the IMF is definitely
something that's attractive

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:08.850
to a lot of countries.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.620
- [Narrator] The New
Development Bank also wants

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.170
to deepen the use of local currencies

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.140
instead of the US dollar
for trade and financing

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.703
between the BRICS nations.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.110
Ultimately, experts say one
of the biggest challenges

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.890
for the BRICS to counter the
west is to form a united voice

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.600
on major global issues.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.480
- Generally, the countries
have pretty diverse

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.830
and diverging interests in
politics, on security issues,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.660
and even relations with the US.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.700
I think the group would like to be seen

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.310
as an alternative political forum

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.310
to the G7 group of developed nations.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.710
Whether or not it is able to achieve that,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.255
I think will largely depend
on what countries it admits.

00:04:50.255 --> 00:04:52.838
(bright music)

